\section{Related work}
\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation of one benchmark.}
A line of work shrinks the number of \emph{items} needed to estimate a model's score on a single benchmark: anchor points \citep{vivek2024anchor}, efficient benchmarking \citep{perlitz2024efficient}, tinyBenchmarks \citep{polo2024tiny}, metabench \citep{kipnis2024metabench} and IRT-based adaptive item selection \citep{fluid2025}. These methods choose items inside a benchmark to recover that benchmark's total. We choose whole benchmarks, and the target is the score on \emph{other} benchmarks.

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark predictability.}
Public leaderboards are low-dimensional: a few capability components computed from many models predict downstream scores \citep{ruan2024observational}, and skill-level scaling laws transfer across families \citep{polo2024sloth}. \citet{perlitz2024benchbench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend on protocol choices. These works take the predictor's input as given (compute, family, or all other scores); they do not treat the choice of the $k$ runs as a design problem, give an error-versus-$k$ law, or test it on families and models released later than the history.

\paragraph{Matrix completion.}
Low-rank completion \citep{candes2009exact,mazumder2010spectral}, probabilistic PCA \citep{tipping1999probabilistic} and probabilistic matrix factorisation \citep{salakhutdinov2007pmf,hernandezlobato2014pmf,udell2016glrm} supply the predictors we compare and the model we fit. They assume the observed cells are random (or model the missingness as a nuisance); here the observed set of the new row is chosen by the evaluator.

\paragraph{Choosing what to observe.}
Cold-start item selection in recommenders \citep{elahi2016active}, column subset selection and CUR decompositions \citep{mahoney2009cur}, and sensor placement \citep{krause2005sensors,ranieri2014framesense} choose which entries to observe. Greedy selection for linear prediction has approximation guarantees through the submodularity ratio \citep{das2011submodular,bian2017greedy}. Our selector is the Bayesian linear-Gaussian instance of this toolkit. What we add is its test on benchmark matrices, where models cluster in families, the new model may lie outside the span of the history, and scales saturate.
EOF
